title: Six tests, run in order
subtitle: Signing and scanning were right on the first apply. The enforcement took five goes.
width: 1150
---
1. A clean image is signed and stays deployable
   [ok]  signature COMPLETE
   [ok]  ALLOW  digest=a366a833...  tags=['clean-20261006-053301']

2. An OS-vulnerable image is quarantined
   [ok]  QUARANTINE(push)  removed=['vuln-os-20261006-053301']
         47 findings, 7 CRITICAL. Caught only after the SECOND trigger existed
         - see the blind spot below.

3. A SIGNED image with a vulnerable library is still quarantined
   [ok]  signature COMPLETE  *and*  quarantined
         This is the whole point. A valid signature says who built the image.
         It says nothing about whether the image is safe to run.

   What was in it - 16 findings on a CURRENT base image:
     PYTHON  12   <- what you are paying Inspector for
     OS       4

     CRITICAL  CVE-2022-29361  werkzeug   fixed in 2.1.1
     HIGH      CVE-2023-30861  flask      fixed in 2.3.2
     HIGH      CVE-2023-25577  werkzeug   fixed in 2.2.3
     HIGH      CVE-2024-34069  werkzeug   fixed in 3.0.3

   ECR's free basic scanner reads OS packages only. It would have reported
   4 findings on this image and called it a day.

4. A repository outside the filter is neither signed nor scanned
   [ok]  "signingStatuses": []
         The filter is a real boundary, not decoration - which matters,
         because every cost control this week rests on it.

5. How long between push and enforcement
   [ok]  pushed 04:32:42Z  ->  quarantined ~04:34
         Scanning is asynchronous. There is a window, roughly 90 seconds here,
         in which an unscanned image is pullable. "We scan on push" is weaker
         than it sounds.

6. The gate fails CLOSED
   [ok]  observed for real, not simulated: the gate broke on a malformed
         repository ARN, raised, and fired its error alarm. Nothing was
         waved through.
         [FAIL] but failing closed is not enforcing - the vulnerable images
         were still deployable, and only test 2 caught that.

--- what the tests found that review did not --------------------------------

  [FAIL] the scan event's "repository-name" is an ARN WITH the digest appended;
         the AWS docs show it without. Every quarantine failed.
  [FAIL] HCP never deployed the fix: the Lambda lives outside the workspace's
         working directory, so the push triggered no run. Committed, pushed,
         green, and running the old code.
  [FAIL] Inspector never re-scans a digest it has seen. Re-tagging an existing
         image - how images normally reach production - produced no event and
         no enforcement at all.
  [FAIL] quarantine removed only the tag named in the event. An image with two
         tags stayed pullable by the other one.
  [FAIL] packageManager is PYTHON for a container image, not PIP. The check
         looking for PIP reported "none found" on a passing test.
